---

<h2 id="guidelines" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">1. Guidelines</h2>

For **new or edited** source files (not trivial one-line fixes):

1. **File header** — Start with a short block that states what the file is for and how it fits the app (one small paragraph or a few lines for Python module docstrings / TS `/** ... */`).
2. **Per-symbol minimum** — [Section 2](#cdcm-per-symbol): **every** class and **every** function/method gets **at least one** doc line (not optional “when non-obvious”).
3. **Inline comments** — **In addition** to per-symbol docs: add line or block comments where behavior is non-obvious, data crosses layers, or a reader would ask “why this way?”. Skip inline noise that only restates the code.
4. **Tone** — Prefer plain language; one-line docstrings are fine; avoid essays on trivial accessors unless they encode a contract.

Applies to Python, TypeScript/TSX, CSS (section headers where layout or intent isn’t obvious), and tests (see [exemptions](#cdcm-per-symbol)).

---

<h2 id="cdcm-per-symbol" style="color:#1565c0;font-size:1.22em;font-weight:650;border-left:4px solid #42a5f5;padding-left:10px;margin-top:1.1em">2. Per-class and per-function minimum</h2>

**Default:** On **new or non-trivially edited** modules touched by product work (including **`implref`** phases), **each** of the following MUST have **≥1 line** of documentation immediately above or on the symbol:

| Symbol | Python | TypeScript / TSX |
|--------|--------|------------------|
| **Class** (incl. dataclass, `@dataclass`, React component) | Class docstring or `#:` on class line | `/** … */` on class or `function Component` |
| **Method / function** (module-level, method, staticmethod, `@classmethod`) | Function docstring (PEP 257 one-liner OK) | `/** … */` before `function` / method |
| **Interface / type alias** (when it names a contract) | N/A or module comment | One-line `/** … */` on the type |

**What the line must convey (pick one or blend briefly):** purpose, inputs/outputs, side effects, caller, invariant, or “why not the obvious alternative.”

**Examples (acceptable one-liners):**

```python
def rrf_list_weights(mode: str | None = None) -> tuple[float, float]:
    """Return (vector_weight, lexical_weight) for reciprocal_rank_fusion."""
```

```typescript
/** POST body for batch verify — mirrors single-unit shape for parity tests. */
export type BatchVerifyRequest = { … };
```

**Exemptions (do not require a new doc line):**

| Case | Rule |
|------|------|
| **Trivial one-line edit** | Whole-file pass not required (same as **implref**). |
| **Nested function / lambda** | Unless >~5 lines or encodes business logic — then one line above. |
| **Generated / vendored trees** | Unless the user includes them in scope. |
| **Pure re-export** | `from x import y  # noqa` shims — file header covers re-exports. |
| **Test functions** | One line naming the **contract under test** (may be the docstring only). |
| **Obvious protocol stubs** | Empty `__init__` that only calls `super()` — class docstring suffices; method need not duplicate. |

**Anti-patterns (fail the bar):**

- Class with methods where **some** have docstrings and **some** have none in the same edited file.
- Doc line that repeats the identifier only (`"""search_ids."""`) with no meaning.
- Using `# TODO: document` instead of a real line before marking **implref** phase `[X]`.

